59:Background: People with bipolar disorder (BD) tend to show widespread cognitive impairment compared to healthy controls. Impairments in processing speed (PS), attention and executive function (EF) may represent 'core' impairments that have a role in wider cognitive dysfunction. Cognitive impairments appear to relate to structural brain abnormalities in BD, but whether core deficits are related to particular brain regions is unclear and much of the research on brain-  cognition associations is limited by univariate analysis and small samples.
61:Methods: Euthymic BD patients ( n = 56)  and  matched  healthy  controls  ( n = 26)  underwent T1-  weighted MRI scans and completed neuropsychological tests of PS, attention and EF. We utilised public datasets to develop normative models of cortical thickness ( n = 5977) to generate robust estimations of cortical abnormalities in patients. Canonical correlation analysis was used to assess multivariate brain-  cognition associations in BD, controlling for age, sex and premorbid IQ.
63:Results: BD showed impairments on tests of PS, attention and EF, and abnormal cortical thickness in several brain regions compared to healthy controls. Impairments in tests of PS and EF were most strongly associated with cortical thickness in the left inferior temporal, right entorhinal and right temporal pole areas.
65:Conclusion: Impairments in PS, attention and EF can be observed in euthymic BD and may be related to abnormal cortical thickness in temporal regions. Future research should continue to leverage normative modelling and multivariate methods to examine  complex  brain-  cognition  associations  in  BD.  Future  research  may  benefit  from exploring covariance between traditional brain structural morphological metrics such as cortical thickness, cortical volume and surface area.
83:People with bipolar disorder (BD) tend to show widespread cognitive impairment compared to healthy controls. 1,2 BD is also associated with structural brain abnormalities, including abnormal cortical thickness, compared to healthy controls, across widespread areas of the brain. 3 Both cognitive impairments and brain abnormalities can be observed in euthymia, suggesting they represent core features or consequences of BD. 2,4 Some studies suggest a relationship between brain structure and cognitive impairment in BD; 4-7 however, there is currently a lack of multivariate studies.
85:Processing speed (PS), attention and executive function (EF) are often considered primary or 'core' cognitive impairments in BD and may explain wider cognitive dysfunction, such as poor memory. 8-10 From this perspective, their potential relationship to abnormal brain morphology may be particularly important. Some research suggests that performance on tests of PS, attention and EF may indeed relate to cortical thickness in BD, even in euthymia. 5,11 Neuropsychological tests  that  capture  these  processes,  such  as  the  Digit  Symbol Substitution Test (DSST) and Trail-  Making Test (TMT), often detect a large magnitude of impairment 12,13 and have been specifically related to cortical thickness in BD. 11 However, findings are inconsistent  with  not  all  studies  detecting  associations. 13-15 Few  previous studies have focussed on the aforementioned cognitive processes, and studies often omit tests of PS and attention or focus instead on general cognitive functioning. Thus, the complex nature of the relationship between core cognitive dysfunction and cortical thickness is not well understood and there is no consensus on the specific brain regions involved.
87:Several challenges currently limit brain-  cognition studies: recent research  suggests  that  brain-  behaviour  associations  are  not  replicable, 16 and results appear inconsistent with some studies failing to  detect  significant  structural  brain-  cognition  associations. 15,17,18 This may be driven by small samples; here, normative modelling approaches offer a powerful tool to mitigate this limitation and provide more robust estimates of brain abnormalities. 19 Previous research generally  relies  on  univariate  analysis,  where  the  relationship  between a single cognitive function and the structure of several brain regions is tested. Studying cognitive functions independently may not accurately model cognitive impairment in BD, since processes likely  interact  and  overlap,  especially  in  the  presence  of  cognitive impairment. 8,9,20,21 The  univariate  approach is also constrained by the problem of multiple comparisons, where there is a risk of missing real associations after correcting for multiple comparisons (i.e., increased type-  II errors). Multivariate methods that can simultaneously accommodate the multivariate cognitive and brain data may better  reflect  the  complex,  multimodal  nature  of  behaviour  and physiology. Such methods are beginning to be leveraged to assess brain-  cognition  relationships 5,22,23 ;  however,  few  studies  have  utilised these methods, especially in BD.
89:We examined multivariate associations between cortical thickness and core cognitive functions in euthymic BD. We used large public neuroimaging datasets to build normative models of cortical thickness to produce robust estimations of structural abnormalities in BD. 19 We hypothesised that abnormalities in cortical thickness in groups of regions would be associated with poor core cognitive performance, particularly in PS and attention.
93:Data  were  collected  as  part  of  the  Bipolar  Lithium  Imaging  and Spectroscopy Study (BLISS). 24 T1-  weighted imaging, diffusionweighted  imaging  and  lithium  imaging  data  have  been  reported previously. 24,25 BLISS was granted a favourable ethical opinion by a United Kingdom National Research Ethics Committee (14/NE/1135). All participants provided written informed consent.
101:BLISS  was  originally  designed  to  investigate  lithium  treatment effects  and  therefore  aimed  to  recruit  a  1:1:1  split  across  three groups: BD taking lithium, BD naïve to lithium and healthy controls. This study is a secondary analysis of the BLISS data, where lithium effects were not the primary focus, therefore, we merged the two BD  groups  to  increase  statistical  power.  Where  appropriate,  we checked for differences between the two BD groups to check for confounding effects of lithium. A total of 32 BD patients were taking lithium; the other patients were taking maintenance treatments but were naïve to lithium.
103:A total of 27 matched healthy controls (HC) were recruited via the  Institute  of  Neuroscience  volunteer  database  at  Newcastle
105:University.  Healthy  controls  had  no  history  of  psychiatric  illness and were not taking any psychotropic medications. All participants were 18-65 years of age. Exclusion criteria for all participants were: contraindications to MR examination (e.g., cardiac pacemaker claustrophobia, exceeding 150 kg in weight); current or past medical condition  likely  to  affect  brain  structure;  current  or  recent  substance abuse  (NetSCID  Module  E);  alcohol  intake  exceeding  21 units  per week  (self-  reported);  and  a  learning  disability  or  impairment  of capacity.
109:Premorbid IQ was measured with the National Adult Reading Test (NART), a commonly used test in which participants are asked to read a list of words aloud, with no time limit imposed. The examiner records whether each word had been pronounced correctly, totalling the number of errors, which can be converted into an IQ score.
111:## 2.2.1  |  Neuropsychological tests
113:A battery of pen-  and-  paper neuropsychological tests 28 was used to measure PS, attention and EF as potential primary cognitive impairments in BD that appear to be related to brain structural abnormalities. 9-11 To  allow  comparisons of our results to previous research, we chose standardised and validated tests that are commonly used in the literature and recommended by the International Society for Bipolar Disorders-Battery for Assessment of Neurocognition. 13,29
115:The DSST measured PS and attention, as it is sensitive to cognitive impairment in euthymic BD, detecting large effect sizes compared to controls. 13,30 Participants transcribed unique geometric symbols with corresponding Arabic numbers using a key on the page. Participants also completed a sub-  test of the DSST, Symbol Copy, which involves copying symbols from one row of boxes to another, retaining elements of motor speed but not requiring other cognitive processes involved in transcribing. We subtracted the time taken to complete the Symbol Copy version from the time taken to complete the DSST original version to reflect cognitive speed without the motor component.
117:The TMT was used as a validated, standardised test that is sensitive to cognitive impairment in BD. 30 Part A (TMT-  A) was used to measure PS and attention. Participants connected a series of numbered circles spread on a page in ascending order. Part B (TMT-  B) measured EF: participants connected a series of circles that either contained a number or letter, in alternate ascending and alphabetical order (e.g.,  1-  A-  2-  B-  3-  C,  etc.).  In  both  parts,  the  time  to  complete the task was measured. TMT-  B tests working memory and switching (elements of EF), as well as motor speed, cognitive speed and attention. The latter three functions were fractioned out of the TMT-  B score by subtracting the time to complete TMT-  A from the time to complete TMT-  B, leaving a measure of EF.
123:controls. 31 Participants named as many animals as possible in 60 sec. The number of correctly named animals was recorded.
125:The d2 test was used to measure effortful attention and concentration, since effortful (but not automatic) attention appears to be impaired in BD. 32 Participants crossed out as many target stimuli as possible (the letter 'd' with two dashes above or below it) amongst a series of distractor stimuli (letters 'd' or 'p' with any other number of dashes around it) within 20 seconds. Three outcome measures were recorded: the percentage of correct responses; the total number of correctly cancelled targets minus the total number incorrectly cancelled (concentration performance; CP); and the maximum minus the minimum of total items processed in a trial (fluctuation rate; FR). CP captures effortful attention and the ability to coordinate speed and accuracy and correlates with reaction times in BD. 32 FR captures inconsistency in performance.
129:All  participants  attended  a  screening  visit  to  obtain  informed  consent  and  assess  eligibility  during  a  clinical  interview.  The  interview included: NetSCID to confirm diagnosis and comorbidities in patients and to screen for psychiatric illness in healthy controls; observer-  rated mood scales (YMRS and HAM-  D) to confirm euthymic mood; and an MR safety questionnaire. Clinical interviews were conducted by an experienced research assistant (CF), supervised by a senior psychiatrist (DAC) via discussion of the assessment and suitability for inclusion.

